course: dictionary
lesson: 4491
title: Words that measure: -ful
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The ending -ful can turn a word for a container into a word for an amount. A spoon holds a spoonful, a hand holds a handful, and a hat holds a hatful. So a hatful is the amount a hat can hold.
explain: Hatful can also mean a large number, especially in everyday talk. «He scored a hatful of goals» does not mean he put goals in a hat. It means he scored lots of goals. Ask yourself: would lots of fit here? If it does, that is the meaning.
explain: In «And Toad wouldn't come here alone for a hatful of gold!», the hat is a picture of a big amount. Toad is saying that he would not come for a great deal of gold, so the words around hatful give you the clue.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: The ending -ful often makes a word that measures an amount: spoonful, handful, cupful, hatful.
fact: A hatful is the amount a hat can hold.
fact: «A hatful of goals» means lots of goals, a more informal use.
fact: Hatful is a noun, and its plural is hatfuls.
exercise: «And Toad wouldn't come here alone for a hatful of gold!» What does a hatful of gold mean here? | *A great deal of gold | A tiny bit of gold | A gold hat | Gold kept in a box
exercise: What does a spoonful mean? | A spoon made of gold | A tool for stirring tea | A big spoon for soup | *The amount a spoon can hold
exercise: «He scored a hatful of goals.» Does this mean he scored lots of goals? | No, he scored no goals at all | *Yes, lots of goals | Yes, but only one goal | No, he put goals in a hat
test: The -ful ending in cupful is used to show what? | A place to sleep | A type of animal | A colour | *An amount
test: In «a hatful of goals», what does hatful mean? | A hat full of goals in a box | *A large number | A small hat | A single goal
exam: What is the plural of hatful? | hatful's | hatfulls | *hatfuls | hatsful
